\title{Parameterised theories}
\import{prelude}

\p{Coln also provides the facility for defining functions at the \em{theory} level, which enables code reuse between database schemas. Here's a simple example:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} ProcessOn (Stage : \kwi{Set}) := \kw{sig}
  next : Stage -> Stage
\kw{end}
}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Factory := \kw{sig}
  Step : \kwi{Set}
  chocolate-recipe : ProcessOn Step
  Room : \kwi{Set}
  boat-ride : ProcessOn Room
\kw{end}
}

\p{In this case, \code{ProcessOn} is not lowered to a schema: it does not make sense to do so for a parameterised theory. Only once it is fully applied in \code{Factory} does it have a sensible schema interpretation. The two uses of \code{ProcessOn} each have their own \code{next} table which references the appropriate \code{Stage} table.}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Step}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.chocolate-recipe.next}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Step}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Step}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{s1}} \cell{\code{s2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s2}} \cell{\code{s3}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{s3}} \cell{\code{s3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Room}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{r1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.boat-ride.next}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Room}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Room}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{r1}} \cell{\code{r2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r2}} \cell{\code{r2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} of \code{root.chocolate-recipe.next} is a row ID in \code{root.Step}.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.Step} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of \code{root.chocolate-recipe.next}.}
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} of \code{root.boat-ride.next} is a row ID in \code{root.Room}.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.Room} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of \code{root.boat-ride.next}.}
  }
}

\p{We can also supply the same input to two uses of a parameterised theory:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} FactoryRoutes := \kw{sig}
  Room : \kwi{Set}
  boat-ride : ProcessOn Room
  glass-elevator : ProcessOn Room
\kw{end}
}

\p{Here, the boat ride and glass elevator have independent \code{next} functions over the same collection \code{Room}.}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.Room}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{r1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.boat-ride.next}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Room}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Room}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{r1}} \cell{\code{r2}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r2}} \cell{\code{r3}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r3}} \cell{\code{r3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.glass-elevator.next}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.Room}} \hcell{\code{b : root.Room}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{r1}} \cell{\code{r3}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r2}} \cell{\code{r1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{r3}} \cell{\code{r3}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in columns \code{a} and \code{b} of both \code{next} tables is a row ID in \code{root.Room}.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.Room} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of each \code{next} table.}
  }
}

\p{Parameters need not be elements of \code{\kwi{Set}}; we can use parameters with any type. We might use this to factor a complicated theory into smaller pieces. Consider the \code{Tour} theory from [[001C]]. We might choose to define a separate theory \code{Outcome} that just captures the outcome of a \code{Tour}, and then a third theory that pairs them together:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Tour := \kw{sig}
  Child : \kwi{Set}
  Adult : \kwi{Set}
\kw{end}

\kw{theory} Outcome (tour : Tour) := \kw{sig}
  ironic-comeuppance : tour.Child -> \kwi{String}
\kw{end}

\kw{theory} Narrative := \kw{sig}
  tour : Tour
  outcome : Outcome tour
\kw{end}
}

\dbexample{
  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.tour.Child}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.tour.Adult}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{Row ID}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{a1}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Table \code{root.outcome.ironic-comeuppance}}}

  \table{
    \row{\hcell{\code{a : root.tour.Child}} \hcell{\code{b : \kwi{String}}}}
  }{
    \row{\cell{\code{c1}} \cell{\code{"blueberry inflation"}}}
    \row{\cell{\code{c2}} \cell{\code{"down the rubbish chute"}}}
  }

  \p{\strong{Constraints:}}
  \ul{
    \li{Each value in column \code{a} of \code{root.outcome.ironic-comeuppance} is a row ID in \code{root.tour.Child}.}
    \li{Every row ID in \code{root.tour.Child} appears exactly once in column \code{a} of \code{root.outcome.ironic-comeuppance}.}
  }
}
